 are extant."}, {"sourceSlug": "phintys", "sourceName": "Phintys", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gender-roles-classical-greece", "targetName": "Gender Roles in Classical Greece", "context": "Phintys's philosophical work provides a prescriptive and descriptive account of ideal female conduct, influencing the understanding of gender roles in the Classical and Hellenistic periods."}], "places": [{"name": "Magna Graecia, Southern Italy", "role": "Probable region of activity"}, {"name": "Alexandria, Egypt", "role": "Center of knowledge preservation"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Major philosophical hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Phintys", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "phintys"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q433919", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phintys"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Phintys holds regional significance as one of the few female philosophers from antiquity whose writings are known, providing crucial insights into ancient ethical thought and women's roles. Her work offers a unique perspective on Pythagoreanism and female virtue that impacts our understanding of Classical intellectual history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-05T18:42:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31035516157", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Phintys was a Pythagorean philosopher, probably from the third century BC. She wrote a work on the correct behaviour of women, two extracts of which are preserved by Stobaeus.", "newValue": "Phintys, a Pythagorean philosopher likely active in the 3rd century BCE, stands as one of the few ancient women philosophers whose writings are known, albeit through fragments. Hailing from the Hellenistic world, possibly Magna Graecia, her significance lies in her contribution to ethical philosophy, specifically her treatise on the proper conduct of women. This work offers a rare glimpse into the intellectual lives and moral expectations placed upon aristocratic women in the Classical and Hellenistic periods, challenging the common perception of women's exclusion from serious philosophical discourse.

Her principal known work, 'On the Correct Behaviour of Women' (Περὶ γυναικὸς σωφροσύνας), survives through two substantial extracts preserved by the 5th-century CE anthologist Stobaeus in his *Anthologium*. These fragments detail virtues such as modesty, temperance, and piety, advocating for a woman's dedication to her household, husband, and children while also emphasizing the importance of intellectual cultivation and adherence to philosophical principles. Phintys's teachings reflect a synthesis of Pythagorean ethics, which valued harmony and order, with the societal norms of her time, providing a philosophical framework for female virtue.

The legacy of Phintys is multifaceted. Her work serves as a crucial primary source for understanding ancient philosophical perspectives on gender roles and female ethics, particularly within the Pythagorean tradition which was notably more open to female participation than many other schools. The very survival of her text, however fragmented, through Stobaeus's compilation, underscores the enduring interest in ethical guidance and the unexpected pathways through which ancient knowledge was transmitted. Her voice, though faint, echoes across millennia, contributing to a more nuanced understanding of intellectual life in antiquity."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-05T18:42:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31035516157", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "400 BCE, Crotone"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "400 BCE, Crotone"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual climate of Pythagoreanism fostered an environment where women could engage in philosophical inquiry.", "The practice of later Greek anthologists like Stobaeus meticulously preserving fragments of earlier philosophical texts ensured the survival of her work.", "The societal need for ethical guidelines regarding gender roles prompted philosophical discourse on women's conduct in the Classical era."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-05T18:42:45Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31035516157", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "300 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "300 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Phintys provided a unique female voice within the ancient philosophical tradition, offering insights 